Reported from IDOL against 0.1.0; reproduced on 0bc4fcb.

`edit -a`, `edit -b` and `new` read the body from stdin and take empty
stdin as an empty body. Each one reports success and exits 0:

- `edit -a N </dev/null` prints `N updated` and appends nothing.
- `edit -b N </dev/null` prints `N updated` and erases the body. The
  half-body guard (README "-b also refuses…") only covers bodies of at
  least 200 bytes, so a short body goes without `-f`. On a 640-byte
  body it refuses correctly.
- `new T </dev/null` creates a ticket with no body.

How it bit: IDOL's agent instructions say to run pidly with `</dev/null`.
An agent that also piped a body in wrote
`printf 'note\n' | pidly edit -a 7 </dev/null`. The later redirect
replaces the pipe, pidly said `7 updated`, and the closing notes on two
tickets were lost. The shell line is the agent's mistake. pidly's part is
reporting success for a write that did nothing, which hid it.

Repro:

```sh
mkdir t && cd t && git init -q && pidly init </dev/null
printf 'The original body, several words long.\n' | pidly new t
pidly edit -a 1 </dev/null; echo "rc=$?"   # 1 updated, rc=0, nothing added
pidly edit -b 1 </dev/null; echo "rc=$?"   # 1 updated, rc=0, body gone
pidly new t2 </dev/null;    echo "rc=$?"   # rc=0, ticket 2 has no body
```

Direction (not decided):

- `edit -a` with empty stdin refuses and exits non-zero. Appending
  nothing is never what was meant, so there is nothing to override.
- `edit -b` and `new` refuse an empty body unless `-f` is given. An empty
  body is a legal ticket, so it has to stay reachable on purpose.

Ask the owner before building: refusing empty stdin changes visible CLI
behaviour. Decide whether `-f` is the right escape for `new`, and whether
"empty" means zero bytes or whitespace only.

Not confirmed: the report says `updated:` is stamped on the no-op `-a`.
The repro ran within one second, so I couldn't see whether it was.
